  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef has binary string SSS of length NNN.\n\nChef can perform the following operation on the string:\n\n\nChoose a contiguous subarray SL,SL+1,…,SRS_L, S_{L + 1}, \\ldots , S_RSL​,SL+1​,…,SR​ such that the count of set bits in the subarray is **equal** to the count of unset bits in the subarray.\nReplace the chosen subarray with either a set bit or an unset bit.\nChef wants to reduce the string to **minimum** possible length using **minimum** number of given operations. Help Chef by telling him the **minimum** length and also the operations required to obtain that. If there are multiple ways to obtain the answer, print any.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN, denoting the length of the binary string.\nThe second line of each test case contains a binary string SSS.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output K+1K+1K+1 lines:\n\n\n\nThe first line should contain two space-separated integers MMM and KKK, denoting the **minimum** length that can be obtained and the **minimum** number of operations required to obtain it respectively.\nThen, KKK lines follow, where the ithi^{th}ith line denotes the ithi^{th}ith operation:\n\nEach operation is denoted using three space separated integers LLL, RRR, and BBB.\n\nThe integers LLL and RRR denote the chosen substring such that (1≤L&lt;R≤∣S∣)(1\\le L\\lt R \\le |S|)(1≤L&lt;R≤∣S∣) and the substring S[L,R]S[L, R]S[L,R] has equal count of set and unset bits. Note that, ∣S∣|S|∣S∣ denotes the length of the current string.\n\nThe integer BBB denotes the bit with which the substring is replaced.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\leq T \\leq 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤N≤10001 \\leq N \\leq 10001≤N≤1000"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n4\n1100\n1\n1\n5\n11000",
        "output": "1 1\n1 4 0\n1 0\n1 2\n1 4 1\n1 2 1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: We can reduce the string to a string of length 111. We require only 111 operation to do so:\n\nChoose L=1,R=4,L = 1, R = 4,L=1,R=4, and B=0B = 0B=0. We chose the substring S[1,4]S[1,4]S[1,4] which contains 222 set bits and 222 unset bits. We can replace the chosen substring with bit 000.\n\nTest case 222: The given string is of length 111. Thus, we cannot reduce it any further.\nTest case 333: We can reduce the string to a string of length 111. We require 222 operations to do so:\n\nOperation 111: Choose L=1,R=4,L = 1, R = 4,L=1,R=4, and B=1B = 1B=1. We chose the substring S[1,4]S[1,4]S[1,4] which contains 222 set bits and 222 unset bits. We can replace the chosen substring with bit 111. Thus, the string after this operation is S=10S = 10S=10.\nOperation 222: Choose L=1,R=2,L = 1, R = 2,L=1,R=2, and B=1B = 1B=1. We chose the substring S[1,2]S[1,2]S[1,2] which contains 111 set bit and 111 unset bit. We can replace the chosen substring with bit 111. Thus, the string after this operation is S=1S = 1S=1."
      }
    ]
